n='nsec-vpns';wg=['https://www.wireguard.com/papers/wireguard.pdf'];quick=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/wg-quick.8.html']
edit(n,'A **VPN** is a tunnel plus cryptography:','Here, **VPN** means a cryptographically protected IP tunnel:','VPNalsoincludesnonencryptedproviderisolation.')
edit(n,'the two VPN protocols you will meet most (IPsec and WireGuard)','two examples of protected VPN protocols (IPsec and WireGuard)','Unverifiedpopularityranking.')
edit(n,'the standard MTUs for GRE, WireGuard (sized for an IPv6 underlay so it works over both) and VXLAN.','inner IP size budgets for these exact header assumptions: basic GRE over IPv4, WireGuard over IPv6, and untagged VXLAN over IPv4. They are not universal interface defaults. Options, VLAN tags, extra tunnels and path MTU change the budget; WireGuard padding is bounded so it does not exceed the selected MTU.','Scopeoverheadmodel.',wg+rfc(7348))
edit(n,'    used = sum(HDR[x] for x in layers)\n    return link_mtu - used','    if type(link_mtu) is not int:\n        raise TypeError("integer MTU required")\n    used = sum(HDR[x] for x in layers)\n    if link_mtu <= used:\n        raise ValueError("no inner capacity")\n    return link_mtu - used','RejectnegativeorzerocapacityandnonintegerMTUs.')
edit(n,'(as WireGuard does by default)','(using the actual underlay budget; wg-quick can infer an initial value)','No universalWG1420default.',quick)
edit(n,'so TCP never builds oversized segments: MSS = MTU − 40 for IPv4 (20 IP + 20 TCP), so 1,380 for a 1,420 tunnel.','to advertise an appropriate data budget. With minimum IPv4/TCP headers, MSS = MTU − 40, giving 1,380 for this 1,420-byte example; senders must account for options, and an MSS clamp does not fix UDP or later path changes.','MSSnotuniversalalltrafficguarantee.',rfc(6691))
edit(n,'adds a 4-byte header and can carry almost any protocol.','has a 4-byte base header, with optional fields adding overhead, and can encapsulate different payload protocols.','GREminimumnotallfixed4.',rfc(2784))
edit(n,'anyone who can send packets to the endpoint can inject them.','forged traffic may be accepted if it reaches an endpoint without effective filtering or other validation.','Noauthnotguaranteedinjection.')
edit(n,'so every application above benefits without changes.','and can protect selected traffic without application changes, according to configured policies.','IPsecdoesnotcoveralltrafficimplicitly.',rfc(4301))
edit(n,'The result is a pair of **Security Associations** (SAs), one per direction, each identified by a 32-bit **SPI**.','IKE establishes a bidirectional IKE SA (with separate 64-bit initiator/responder SPIs) and negotiates Child SAs for data traffic. An ESP Child-SA pair contains unidirectional SAs with 32-bit SPIs.','DistinguishIKEandESPsecurityassociations.',rfc(7296))
edit(n,"it encrypts and authenticates each packet using the SA's keys.","it protects the selected data packets using the negotiated SA algorithms. The encrypted-and-authenticated examples here use AEAD; ESP also permits other protection combinations, including integrity without encryption.",'ESPnotalwaysencryption.',rfc(4303))
edit(n,'AH covers the outer IP header too, so any NAT that rewrites addresses breaks it; it is rarely used.','AH also authenticates relevant immutable IP-header fields; mutable fields receive special treatment. Address-rewriting NAT normally conflicts with that authentication.','AHnotentiremutableheaderandunverifiedrare.',rfc(4302))
edit(n,'ESP tunnel mode with AES-GCM adds about 54 to 57 bytes over IPv4:','With a 16-byte tag, no extra options or traffic-flow padding, ESP tunnel mode using AES-GCM adds 54 to 57 bytes over IPv4:','Tagandpaddingparametersrequired.',rfc(4106))
edit(n,'ESP has no ports, so NAT devices that multiplex by port cannot handle it.','ESP has no TCP/UDP ports, complicating port-based NAT multiplexing; some devices implement ESP-specific passthrough.','NotimpossibleallNAT.',rfc(3948))
edit(n,'        self.size = size','        if type(size) is not int or size < 1:\n            raise ValueError("positive size")\n        self.size = size','Rejectinvalidwindowsize.')
edit(n,'    def accept(self, seq):\n        if seq <= self.top - self.size:','    def accept(self, seq):\n        if type(seq) is not int or seq < 1:\n            return False\n        if seq <= self.top - self.size:','ESPtoypositiveintegersequenceonly.')
edit(n,'Real implementations use a bitmap instead of a set.','This toy handles already authenticated positive sequence numbers for one SA, without wrapping. Never update replay state before integrity verification: a forged high counter could otherwise evict legitimate packets. Its set pruning costs O(window size) per accepted packet. Efficient implementations can use bitmaps.','Criticalstatepoisoningpreconditionandcostmodel.',rfc(4303))
edit(n,'| Code size | Very large | Under ~4,000 lines |','| Configuration model | Many suites and policy choices | Fixed cryptographic suite |','Unspecifiedimplementation/versionlinecountunsupported.')
edit(n,'That removes downgrade attacks and most of the configuration that goes wrong with IPsec.','This removes cipher-suite negotiation as a downgrade surface, while key distribution, routing, access policy and implementation security still matter.','Notallattacks/configmistakeseliminated.',wg)
edit(n,'Sessions are re-keyed every two minutes, giving forward secrecy over short periods.','Rekeying uses message and time thresholds. For example, an active initiator triggers a new handshake when sending after a session key reaches the 120-second threshold; this is not a guarantee of a handshake every two minutes while idle.','Traffic/initiatorconditions.',wg+['https://www.wireguard.com/protocol/'])
edit(n,'Each peer is a public key with a list of **AllowedIPs**. That one list does two jobs:','Each peer is a public key with **AllowedIPs** prefixes. OS routing must first select the WireGuard interface; its cryptokey table then selects a peer. The following is a wg-quick-style configuration; the wrapper normally installs routes derived from AllowedIPs:','SeparateOSroutefrompeerselection.',quick+wg)
edit(n,'a packet for `10.20.3.4` is encrypted to the peer whose AllowedIPs contains it.','a packet routed into WireGuard selects the peer by longest-prefix match on its destination in the cryptokey table.','Overlappingprefixselection.',wg)
edit(n,"a decrypted packet is accepted only if its *inner* source address is in the sending peer's AllowedIPs. A peer cannot spoof another peer's addresses.","after authentication, the inner source address must select that same sending peer in the cryptokey table. Merely matching one of its broader prefixes is insufficient if a more-specific prefix selects a different peer.",'Globalpeerlookupvscontains.',wg)
edit(n,'`AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0` would send everything (a **full tunnel**).','With suitable OS routes, `AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0` supports a **full tunnel** for ordinary IPv4/IPv6 destinations. Endpoint, local and policy-route exceptions still need handling.','NotalltrafficroutesfromAllowedIPsalone.',quick)
edit(n,'`PersistentKeepalive` sends a packet every 25 seconds so that a NAT in front of the laptop keeps its mapping open','`PersistentKeepalive = 25` enables periodic authenticated empty packets when needed during idle periods, intended to keep a NAT mapping open when its timeout and policy permit','IdlekeepalivenotabsoluteNATguarantee.',['https://www.wireguard.com/quickstart/'])
edit(n,'WireGuard is also **silent**: it does not respond at all to packets that do not authenticate, so a port scan cannot even tell it is there.','WireGuard avoids replies to random invalid probes. This is not absolute invisibility: under load, a packet with a valid MAC derived from the responder\'s public key can elicit a cookie reply before full peer authentication. Other network behavior can also leak information.','Cookiepublickeynotprivatepeerauthentication.',wg)
edit(n,'That is 50 bytes of overhead, so either the underlay MTU is raised','For the inner IP budget, subtract outer IPv4 (20), UDP (8), VXLAN (8), and inner Ethernet (14): 50 bytes. The outer Ethernet header shown above is outside the underlay IP MTU. Under these assumptions, either the underlay MTU is raised','FixambiguousouterEthversusinnerEthaccounting.',rfc(7348))
edit(n,'Modern fabrics learn which MAC lives behind which VTEP using **BGP EVPN** rather than flooding.','Fabrics can use **BGP EVPN** to distribute MAC reachability, reducing flood-based learning; broadcast, unknown-unicast and multicast handling may still involve replication or flooding.','EVPNdoesnoteliminateallflooding.',rfc(7432))
edit(n,"Anyone who can send UDP to port 4789 on a VTEP can inject frames into any tenant's segment by choosing its VNI.","An untrusted sender may inject frames if a VTEP accepts its packets and the selected VNI is configured. The VNI alone is not authentication; underlay filtering and endpoint policy can reject the traffic.",'NotanyVNIanytenantunconditional.',rfc(7348))
edit(n,'Every tunnel costs MTU (GRE 1,476, VXLAN 1,450, WireGuard 1,420 on a 1,500 link); blocked ICMP turns that into PMTU black holes, fixed by correct MTUs and MSS clamping.','Encapsulation reduces the inner no-fragmentation budget. The worked 1,500-byte examples yield 1,476, 1,450 and 1,420 under their specified headers; actual paths differ, and MSS clamping covers only TCP.','NotuniversalMTUsorfixalltraffic.')
